      "text": "Redis",
      "fontSize": 28,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Redis",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "QHV4h7nWb4T-xmWnucevP",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 952.5949726866977,
      "y": 538.6881449450524,
      "width": 854.1337198582927,
      "height": 325,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b7E",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 478933582,
      "version": 498,
      "versionNonce": 1455780750,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1791300669091,
      "created": 1791300468319,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "- Redis = In-memory data store → stores data mainly in RAM.\n- Very fast because RAM access is much faster than disk/database access.\n- Commonly used as a cache / key-value store.\n- Internally, you can think of it as a HashMap-like store: key → value.\n- Example: chai_price → 20\n- 10,000 users can ask: “What is the price of chai?”\n- Instead of hitting the main DB 10,000 times, they can get 20 directly from Redis.\n- Database → Redis → Users reduces database load.\n- Redis can also persist data to disk, so data can be restored after a Redis restart.\n- On restart, Redis can load persisted data back into memory.\n- Common uses: Caching, sessions, OTPs, rate limiting, counters, queues/pub-sub.\n- Redis is not necessarily a replacement for your main DB; it is often used alongside\nMongoDB/PostgreSQL/MySQL.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "- Redis = In-memory data store → stores data mainly in RAM.\n- Very fast because RAM access is much faster than disk/database access.\n- Commonly used as a cache / key-value store.\n- Internally, you can think of it as a HashMap-like store: key → value.\n- Example: chai_price → 20\n- 10,000 users can ask: “What is the price of chai?”\n- Instead of hitting the main DB 10,000 times, they can get 20 directly from Redis.\n- Database → Redis → Users reduces database load.\n- Redis can also persist data to disk, so data can be restored after a Redis restart.\n- On restart, Redis can load persisted data back into memory.\n- Common uses: Caching, sessions, OTPs, rate limiting, counters, queues/pub-sub.\n- Redis is not necessarily a replacement for your main DB; it is often used alongside MongoDB/PostgreSQL/MySQL.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
