    {
      "id": "-5NcJ2g346BS-hgMqGdhw",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1791.618822894985,
      "y": 1779.8633095945847,
      "width": 819.6517685925378,
      "height": 867.5395381328489,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b8p",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 873477084,
      "version": 657,
      "versionNonce": 1943299420,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1791385550287,
      "created": 1791383874674,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Cache\n- Redis stores frequently accessed data in RAM.\n- Client → Redis → Response\n- Cache HIT → return data from Redis.\n- Cache MISS → get data from DB → store in Redis → return.\n- Reduces DB load and improves speed.\n\nSession Store\n- Redis can store user sessions centrally.\n- Multiple servers can access the same session.\n- Example: Server A / Server B → Redis\n- Useful when using multiple backend servers.\n- Prevents session data from being tied to one server.\n\nOTP Store\n- Redis is useful for storing temporary OTPs.\n- Example: OTP = 2514235\n- Set TTL = 2 minutes.\n- After TTL expires, Redis automatically removes the OTP.\n- Useful for OTP verification, password reset, email verification, etc.\n\nRate Limiting\n- Redis can track how many requests a user/IP has made.\n- Example: IP → count: 6 → TTL: 10 min\n- If the limit is exceeded → block/reject requests.\n- TTL can also act as a cooldown period.\n- Useful for preventing API abuse and excessive requests.\n\nJob Queue\n- Redis can be used as a Job Queue to store tasks that need to be processed later.\n- Example: Add 10 email jobs to a Redis list/queue.\n- App → Redis Queue → Workers\n- Worker = separate app/process (e.g., our MERN/Node.js app) responsible for processing jobs.\n- Workers take jobs from the queue one by one and send the emails.\n- Multiple workers can process jobs in parallel, making the system faster.\n- Useful for emails, notifications, background tasks, image processing, etc.",
      "fontSize": 19.278656402952198,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 6,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Cache\n- Redis stores frequently accessed data in RAM.\n- Client → Redis → Response\n- Cache HIT → return data from Redis.\n- Cache MISS → get data from DB → store in Redis → return.\n- Reduces DB load and improves speed.\n\nSession Store\n- Redis can store user sessions centrally.\n- Multiple servers can access the same session.\n- Example: Server A / Server B → Redis\n- Useful when using multiple backend servers.\n- Prevents session data from being tied to one server.\n\nOTP Store\n- Redis is useful for storing temporary OTPs.\n- Example: OTP = 2514235\n- Set TTL = 2 minutes.\n- After TTL expires, Redis automatically removes the OTP.\n- Useful for OTP verification, password reset, email verification, etc.\n\nRate Limiting\n- Redis can track how many requests a user/IP has made.\n- Example: IP → count: 6 → TTL: 10 min\n- If the limit is exceeded → block/reject requests.\n- TTL can also act as a cooldown period.\n- Useful for preventing API abuse and excessive requests.\n\nJob Queue\n- Redis can be used as a Job Queue to store tasks that need to be processed later.\n- Example: Add 10 email jobs to a Redis list/queue.\n- App → Redis Queue → Workers\n- Worker = separate app/process (e.g., our MERN/Node.js app) responsible for processing jobs.\n- Workers take jobs from the queue one by one and send the emails.\n- Multiple workers can process jobs in parallel, making the system faster.\n- Useful for emails, notifications, background tasks, image processing, etc.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
